{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent underprediction in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show a clear upswing (positive slope), add an upward bias of ~+1.5 to +3.0 €/t to the forecast for the next ~1–15 days (model is consistently too low during rallies).",
    "Turning-point lag: after a sharp drop begins (regime shift from up to down), the model stays too high and too smooth. Apply a faster mean-reversion/downshift: subtract ~1–2 €/t for days ~10–30 when recent momentum flips negative (last 3–7 day return < 0).",
    "Over-optimism at long horizon after peaks: when recent prices are elevated (e.g., >20-day mean by ~1–2 std) the model projects continued strength, but realized paths revert lower by weeks 3–4. Damp long-horizon forecasts toward the 20–30 day mean (shrink deviations by ~30–50% for days 15–30).",
    "Volatility underestimation: the model’s path is too flat vs realized swings. Increase responsiveness by scaling forecast day-to-day changes up (e.g., multiply predicted daily deltas by ~1.2–1.4) but cap extremes to avoid runaway.",
    "Spike handling: after upside spikes, the model underreacts immediately (next ~1–7 days too low), but then overshoots persistence (weeks 3–4 too high). Use a two-stage adjustment: boost near-term (+1–3 €/t for days 1–7) and reduce medium/long-term (−1–3 €/t for days 15–30)."
  ]
}
